      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pittsville was named after William Pitt, Earl of Chatham, honoring his support during the Stamp Act crisis.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pittsville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 1860s, miners found brown hematite iron ore, leading to the 1878 charter of the Franklin and Pittsylvania Railroad to haul ore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pittsville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Pittsville offers a historic general store, Woody's Country Store, and a post office, reflecting its small-community charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pittsville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town sits near scenic views of Smith Mountain, Peaks of Otter, and the Blue Ridge Mountains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pittsville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pittsylvania County, where Pittsville lies, once produced over six billion pounds of tobacco annually by 1840, the highest in Virginia.",
